1. She suffered from a prolonged illness lasting two months, described as being bed-bound with a flu. (Verbatim: "the time i got a fever for like like two months i was i was literally like bed bound with a flu")

2. She was diagnosed with anemia after a program check revealed her lower eyelid was "basically white." (Verbatim: "mine were like basically white kicking glowing and they were like wait a sec... you anemic and I was like am I am I anemic")

3. She avoided going to doctors due to her family’s belief that medical visits were unnecessary unless one was near death. (Verbatim: "if you're not dead you don't get to go to the doctors... if you're still able to breathe and move and function you don't get to go")

4. She has a history of vivid and frequent nightmares, which used to occur every night. (Verbatim: "I used to have like like nightmares every night and now nowadays the fact that I'm dreaming of of streaming and cleaning is much much much much much much much much much nicer")

5. She identifies as a people pleaser, which contributed to her delaying medical care. (Verbatim: "i'm a people pleaser right... i don't want to bother the doctor like like you know i'm kind of dying i don't want to bother you too much though")

6. She participated in a university outreach program involving medical training, including GP checks and simulated surgeries. (Verbatim: "we were going over um like a lot of like we did like things like surgery on oranges and and yeah um then we also did how a regular gp check is done")

7. She took paracetamol to manage symptoms when feeling unwell, but avoided streaming if it required relying on medication. (Verbatim: "I took like um some paracetamol to help but I didn't um like yeah like you know I only have your counsel if I really really really really really have to")

8. She has a strong emotional reaction to canceling streams, feeling deep sadness over it. (Verbatim: "i've freaking cried more like cried even more if i had to cancel")

9. She struggles with feeling like her daily accomplishments are insufficient, even after completing significant tasks. (Verbatim: "i felt like i haven't done enough today and i was like no way i i can release i needed to remind myself i was like i released the cover damn it like that should be it")

10. She experienced difficulty accessing healthcare due to personal and systemic hesitations, despite available free services. (Verbatim: "that's why it took so long for me to go um to what do you call it to to get my anemia sorted") [end of text]



